WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.257
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:08.932
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.232 --> 00:00:16.761
Unit seven: Aesthetic Philosophy. Today you will debate beauty using abstract philosophical debate structures.

4
00:00:17.061 --> 00:00:19.678
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:23.588
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:25.381
Sublime.

7
00:00:26.383 --> 00:00:30.867
Sublime means an experience of vastness or power that overwhelms and awes.

8
00:00:31.483 --> 00:00:34.121
Standing before the glacier, he felt the sublime.

9
00:00:34.717 --> 00:00:37.357
Your turn. Say: Sublime.

10
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.259
Sublime.

11
00:00:42.017 --> 00:00:43.320
Aesthetic judgment.

12
00:00:44.317 --> 00:00:48.394
Aesthetic judgment means an evaluation of something as beautiful or ugly.

13
00:00:48.983 --> 00:00:53.026
An aesthetic judgment feels personal, yet we expect others to agree.

14
00:00:53.617 --> 00:00:56.664
Your turn. Say: Aesthetic judgment.

15
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.058
Aesthetic judgment.

16
00:01:01.817 --> 00:01:02.725
Formalism.

17
00:01:03.717 --> 00:01:08.723
Formalism means the view that an artwork's value lies in its form, not its content.

18
00:01:09.317 --> 00:01:12.814
Formalism praised the painting's composition and ignored its subject.

19
00:01:13.417 --> 00:01:15.953
Your turn. Say: Formalism.

20
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:19.824
Formalism.

21
00:01:20.583 --> 00:01:21.724
Mimesis.

22
00:01:22.717 --> 00:01:26.934
Mimesis means the imitation or representation of reality in art.

23
00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:30.780
Aristotle regarded mimesis as the basis of poetry.

24
00:01:31.383 --> 00:01:34.036
Your turn. Say: Mimesis.

25
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.075
Mimesis.

26
00:01:38.817 --> 00:01:40.445
Aesthetic experience.

27
00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:45.981
Aesthetic experience means the special state of attention aroused by beauty or art.

28
00:01:46.583 --> 00:01:50.603
A concert can offer an aesthetic experience that words cannot capture.

29
00:01:51.217 --> 00:01:54.751
Your turn. Say: Aesthetic experience.

30
00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.414
Aesthetic experience.

31
00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:01.035
Taste.

32
00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:05.965
Taste means the ability to judge what is beautiful or of good quality.

33
00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:09.629
Hume argued that true taste is refined by experience.

34
00:02:10.217 --> 00:02:12.602
Your turn. Say: Taste.

35
00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:16.305
Taste.

36
00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:18.562
Beauty as subjective.

37
00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:23.163
Beauty as subjective means the view that beauty depends on the perceiver.

38
00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:27.294
Beauty as subjective explains why people admire different things.

39
00:02:27.883 --> 00:02:30.849
Your turn. Say: Beauty as subjective.

40
00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.154
Beauty as subjective.

41
00:02:35.917 --> 00:02:37.498
Artistic autonomy.

42
00:02:38.483 --> 00:02:44.117
Artistic autonomy means the freedom of art from moral, political or commercial purpose.

43
00:02:44.717 --> 00:02:47.842
Artistic autonomy was her defence against censorship.

44
00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:51.683
Your turn. Say: Artistic autonomy.

45
00:02:54.533 --> 00:02:56.405
Artistic autonomy.

46
00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.186
Sublimity.

47
00:02:59.183 --> 00:03:02.959
Sublimity means the quality of being grand and awe-inspiring.

48
00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:06.444
The sublimity of the mountains left the poet speechless.

49
00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:09.540
Your turn. Say: Sublimity.

50
00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:13.459
Sublimity.

51
00:03:14.217 --> 00:03:15.171
Perception.

52
00:03:16.183 --> 00:03:19.425
Perception means the way something is sensed and interpreted.

53
00:03:20.017 --> 00:03:23.224
Perception is shaped by culture as well as by the senses.

54
00:03:23.817 --> 00:03:26.063
Your turn. Say: Perception.

55
00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:29.936
Perception.

56
00:03:30.733 --> 00:03:34.674
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:35.517 --> 00:03:40.387
Visal, my friend says Angkor Wat is objectively beautiful. Isn't beauty just opinion?

58
00:03:41.483 --> 00:03:52.832
Whether beauty is objective remains a central aesthetic question. Kant held that judgments of taste claim universal agreement, despite being felt personally.

59
00:03:53.917 --> 00:03:56.752
How can a personal feeling claim universality?

60
00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:06.911
That is precisely the puzzle. What distinguishes a judgment of beauty from a preference for ice cream is that we expect others to share it.

61
00:04:08.017 --> 00:04:09.180
And if they don't?

62
00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:22.131
Then, according to Hume, either their taste is undeveloped or ours is. Whether such a standard exists at all is where the debate really begins.

63
00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:24.686
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:29.941
Grammar focus: Abstract philosophical debate structures.

65
00:04:30.241 --> 00:04:31.393
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:32.083 --> 00:04:35.360
Whether beauty is objective remains a central aesthetic question.

67
00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:39.097
What makes a judgment aesthetic is still disputed.

68
00:04:39.983 --> 00:04:43.051
That taste can be educated raises a further question.

69
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:52.957
Frame a debate with 'Whether…', 'What…', 'That…' as subject, then a verb of status: remains, is disputed, raises, poses. Singular verb after the clause.

70
00:04:53.717 --> 00:04:57.890
Be careful! Don't say: Whether beauty is objective remain open.

71
00:04:59.833 --> 00:05:02.773
Say: Whether beauty is objective remains open.

72
00:05:03.073 --> 00:05:05.363
A noun clause takes a singular verb.

73
00:05:06.167 --> 00:05:08.784
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.309
In the Eye of the Beholder?.

75
00:05:11.609 --> 00:05:16.418
Aesthetics investigates the nature of beauty, art and the experiences they provoke.

76
00:05:17.433 --> 00:05:26.770
Subjectivists claim that beauty exists only in the perceiver, whereas objectivists argue that certain forms, proportions or qualities are beautiful regardless of opinion.

77
00:05:27.767 --> 00:05:34.526
Kant attempted to reconcile the two positions by arguing that aesthetic judgment is felt individually yet demands universal assent.

78
00:05:35.533 --> 00:05:42.444
Formalism values the arrangement of line and colour, while the theory of mimesis values the faithful representation of reality.

79
00:05:43.433 --> 00:05:50.112
Whether any account can fully capture the sublime, the awe aroused by vastness, may itself be a sign of its power.

80
00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:55.074
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:55.917 --> 00:05:58.415
blank beauty is objective remains disputed.

82
00:05:58.715 --> 00:06:02.322
A, Whether. B, Unless. C, Despite.

83
00:06:05.867 --> 00:06:07.971
The answer is A: Whether.

84
00:06:09.117 --> 00:06:11.952
The experience of overwhelming vastness is the blank.

85
00:06:12.252 --> 00:06:16.242
A, mimesis. B, sublime. C, formalism.

86
00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:22.032
The answer is B: sublime.

87
00:06:23.183 --> 00:06:25.763
The imitation of reality in art is blank.

88
00:06:26.063 --> 00:06:29.682
A, taste. B, perception. C, mimesis.

89
00:06:33.233 --> 00:06:35.303
The answer is C: mimesis.

90
00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:38.159
Choose the correct sentence.

91
00:06:38.459 --> 00:06:45.282
A, What makes art great are debated.. B, What makes art great is debated.. C, What make art great is debated..

92
00:06:48.833 --> 00:06:51.878
The answer is B: What makes art great is debated..

93
00:06:53.017 --> 00:06:56.943
According to the reading, Kant argued that aesthetic judgment is felt…

94
00:06:57.243 --> 00:07:04.159
A, individually yet demands universal assent. B, only by experts. C, never consistently.

95
00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:11.778
The answer is A: individually yet demands universal assent.

96
00:07:12.967 --> 00:07:16.397
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:21.281
Is beauty objective, or is it entirely in the eye of the beholder?

98
00:07:38.083 --> 00:07:39.769
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:40.069 --> 00:08:08.748
I think beauty is partly subjective and partly shared. Whether a sunset or a temple moves us depends on our history, yet many different cultures are moved by similar things, which suggests some common human response. Taste can also be educated: the more we study music, the more we notice. So beauty is not purely in the eye of the beholder. It arises in the meeting of an object and a trained perception.

100
00:08:09.048 --> 00:08:10.873
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:11.783 --> 00:08:17.245
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and abstract philosophical debate structures.

102
00:08:17.545 --> 00:08:22.636
Today's value is appreciation of beauty. Learn to notice and value the beauty around you.

103
00:08:22.936 --> 00:08:25.205
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

104
00:08:25.505 --> 00:08:28.180
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

